    - target: {fileID: 8380166838820477280, guid: 439cc8ee763d24b4fba272ad2893e339, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: Soil erosion and the transport of sediment into underground caves through
        surface openings (called sinkholes or dolines) are natural processes that
        have helped shape these environments over thousands of years. As sediments
        move through the cave system, they can accumulate on the ground, affect water
        clarity, and influence how water flows through the underground passages.
        The natural chemical composition of the groundwater also plays an important
        role in the gradual dissolution of limestone and the formation of caves.
